const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Biostatistics and Statistical Programming Service","serviceType":"Biostatistics consulting, CDISC statistical programming and clinical trial analysis","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/biostatistics-and-statistical-programming-services/","description":"Sample size, analysis plans, CDISC SDTM and ADaM, SAS and R programming a reviewer can re-run. Verification $600, analysis $1,235, or $150 an hour.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How does statistical programming streamline complex clinical data analysis?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By turning the analysis into something that runs rather than something that is done. Cleaning, derivation and output generation are written once as code, which means they can be re-run in minutes when a data query resolves, a site's records arrive late or a reviewer asks for one more sensitivity analysis — work that takes days by hand and introduces a new opportunity for error every time. It also makes the analysis reproducible, which matters more than speed: a number produced by a script with a logged version can be produced again in two years, and a number produced by a sequence of manual steps in a spreadsheet usually cannot. The efficiency is real, but reproducibility is the reason regulators and journals now ask for the code."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the best practices for accuracy and consistency in statistical programming?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Independent double programming is the one that catches the most: a second programmer produces every output from the analysis plan, not from the first programmer's code, and the two are reconciled before release. Around one output in twenty will disagree, and roughly a third of those errors will be in the primary programmer's work — which is the entire argument for the practice. Beyond it: version control on every programme with a change log; standardised, reviewed macros rather than code copied between studies; data validation and edit checks before analysis rather than after; documentation written for the person who inherits the study; and peer review of the statistical decisions, not only the syntax. None of this is exotic. It is the difference between an analysis that is probably right and one that is demonstrably right."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between SDTM and ADaM, and do I need both?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"SDTM organises what was collected, in a standard structure a reviewer already knows how to navigate. ADaM structures what will be analysed, adding the derived variables, flags and populations the statistical analysis plan calls for — and crucially preserving traceability, so that every value in a table can be followed back through ADaM to the SDTM record it came from. If you are submitting to the FDA, EMA or PMDA you need both, along with Define.xml describing them. If you are publishing in a journal you need neither, and building them would be an expensive way to satisfy nobody. The honest question is not which standard is better but where the study is going, and we ask that before quoting."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you use R instead of SAS for a regulatory submission?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Increasingly yes, and with more care. No major agency mandates SAS — what they require is that submitted analyses can be reproduced and understood, and R satisfies that when the environment is documented: package versions pinned, the session recorded, and the analysis delivered as scripts rather than as exported results. Several R-based submissions have now gone through successfully. The practical caveats are that reviewers see far more SAS and so read it faster, that some sponsors' internal standard operating procedures still specify SAS, and that a few derived outputs have well-worn SAS procedures with no exact R equivalent. Our usual answer on a submission study is SAS for the regulated deliverables and R for modelling, simulation and figures, which is also what most large sponsors do."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you handle missing data?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"With a strategy named in the analysis plan before the extent of the missingness is known, because choosing afterwards is choosing the answer. Complete-case analysis is the default in a great deal of published work and is also the weakest option: it is unbiased only if data are missing completely at random, which is rarely plausible and almost never tested. Multiple imputation is usually the better primary approach, with the imputation model including the outcome and the variables that predict missingness, and mixed models for repeated measures handle certain patterns without imputation at all. What matters most is the sensitivity analysis: a tipping-point or pattern-mixture analysis showing how far the missing data would have to differ before the conclusion changes. Last observation carried forward should have been retired a decade ago and still appears; we will not use it without a written argument from you."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is an estimand, and why does ICH E9(R1) keep coming up?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"An estimand is a precise statement of what the trial is measuring, and the reason it now appears in every protocol is that studies were being designed without one. Consider a trial where some participants stop treatment because of side effects. Is the question the effect of being assigned to the treatment, whatever happens afterwards, or the effect of actually taking it? Those are different quantities, they give different numbers, and the analysis that answers one does not answer the other. The E9(R1) addendum asks you to say which one you mean before you start, by specifying the population, the endpoint, how intercurrent events like discontinuation and rescue medication are handled, and the population-level summary. It is bureaucratic to write and it prevents a specific, common and expensive argument at review."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you help after the data is already collected?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is a large part of what we do. Be aware of what changes. With the data in hand, the analysis can still be done properly — correctly specified, validated, reported honestly — but it cannot be made pre-specified after the fact, and an analysis plan written once the results are visible is not the same object however carefully it is worded. We will write and date one anyway, describe it accurately as post hoc where it is, and say what the data can and cannot support. Occasionally that means telling you the study is under-powered for its primary question, which is unwelcome and is still better learned from us than from a reviewer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you manage large datasets and reproducible workflows?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The same way regardless of size, which is the point: if the workflow only works for small data it was never a workflow. Every step from raw extract to final output is code, run in order from a single entry point, so the whole analysis regenerates from scratch rather than depending on steps somebody remembers performing. Everything is under version control with a change log. Software and package versions are recorded. Intermediate datasets are derived, never hand-edited. Long-running steps are separated so a re-run does not repeat them unnecessarily. For genuinely large data — registries, claims, imaging-derived features — the work moves to a database or a chunked pipeline, but the principle does not change. The test is simple: can somebody else, on a different machine, produce your numbers from your data and your code."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does good programming improve the visualisation and interpretation of results?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because the figure is generated from the analysis rather than rebuilt from it. A Kaplan-Meier curve plotted directly from the ADaM dataset carries the same numbers as the table beside it, with the numbers at risk, the censoring marks and the confidence bands that let a reader judge it — where a chart rebuilt by hand in a spreadsheet drifts from the table the moment either is revised, and that mismatch is something reviewers notice. Programmed figures are also reproducible and restylable: the same code produces the submission version, the journal version at the required resolution and the conference version, without anyone re-reading values off a screen. Good visualisation shows the uncertainty rather than hiding it, and that is a decision made in code."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who is named as an author, and how is our data protected?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On data: participant data is handled under HIPAA and GDPR, with de-identification checked rather than assumed, transfer and storage encrypted, access limited to the team on your study, retention agreed in writing and nothing ever used to train anything. Pubrica’s quality management system is certified to ISO 9001:2015. On authorship: ICMJE criteria require substantial contribution to conception or design, or to acquisition, analysis or interpretation; drafting or critically revising; approval of the final version; and accountability for it. A statistician who designed the analysis and interpreted the results frequently meets all four, and where that is the case we say so and let you decide — rather than accepting an acknowledgement for work that was not acknowledgement-sized, which is a form of ghost authorship whichever direction it runs in. Any AI assistance in programming or drafting is declared."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How many participants do I need?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The honest answer is that the arithmetic is the easy part and the assumptions are the argument. A two-group calculation needs the effect worth detecting, the variability, the power and the significance level; you can run that yourself in the free sample size calculator on this site, which shows the formula it used. What it cannot do is tell you whether the effect you entered is clinically worth detecting, which is the question a reviewer will actually ask, or handle clustering, interim analyses, non-inferiority margins, repeated measures or competing risks — each of which changes the number materially and none of which can be fixed by inflating it afterwards."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"My p-value is 0.06. What now?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Nothing statistical. It is 0.06, and that is not a state of affairs that improves with additional analyses. What you can do is report the estimate and its confidence interval and let the reader see what the study actually supports — which is frequently a range that includes effects worth having and effects that are not. What you should not do is add covariates until it moves, switch to a one-sided test, drop an outlier that was in the plan, or describe it as approaching significance, all of which are visible to a statistical reviewer and all of which cost more credibility than the result was worth. If the interval is wide, the honest conclusion is that the study was too small."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you do the power analysis for a grant application?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is one of the cheaper things to get right in a proposal. A panel reads the sample size section to see whether you understand your own design, so what earns the marks is not the number but the justification: where the effect size came from, why it is the smallest difference worth detecting clinically, what attrition is assumed and on what basis, and what happens to the study if recruitment falls short. We write it as a defensible paragraph with the calculation reproducible from what is printed, and we will tell you if the design cannot support the aims at a feasible sample size — before four months goes into the writing rather than after."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between intention-to-treat and per-protocol?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Intention-to-treat analyses people in the group they were randomised to, whatever happened afterwards. Per-protocol analyses only those who followed the protocol. They answer different questions: intention-to-treat estimates the effect of offering the treatment, which is the pragmatic question and preserves randomisation; per-protocol estimates the effect of receiving it, and breaks randomisation by conditioning on post-randomisation behaviour, which is why it can be badly biased. For a superiority trial intention-to-treat is the primary analysis and per-protocol supports it. For a non-inferiority trial the relationship inverts, because intention-to-treat dilutes towards no difference and therefore towards the conclusion you were trying to establish. ICH E9(R1) is the framework for saying which you mean."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you do interim analyses and sample size re-estimation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and both have to be specified before the data are looked at. An unplanned interim look inflates the type I error rate and cannot be corrected for afterwards; a planned one uses a spending function — O’Brien-Fleming or Pocock, chosen for the stopping behaviour you want — so that the overall error rate is preserved and the boundary is known in advance. Blinded sample size re-estimation, where the nuisance parameter is updated without looking at the treatment effect, is the safer option and is often the right one when the variance assumption was the weak part of the original calculation. Both belong in the protocol and in the analysis plan, dated."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Research services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Biostatistics and statistical programming","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/biostatistics-and-statistical-programming-services/"}]}];

